<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Inference Efficiency</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix E Inference Efficiency ‣ Persona-Pruner: Sculpting Lightweight Models for Role-Playing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> reports parameter counts and inference FLOPs for Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct at 25% sparsity.
We separately report prefill-only FLOPs and prefill-plus-decode FLOPs, where the latter includes autoregressive decoding and therefore depends on generated output length.
Since all pruning baselines are configured to retain at least as many parameters as Persona-Pruner, Persona-Pruner has the smallest parameter count and the lowest prefill-only TFLOPs.
For prefill-plus-decode FLOPs, Persona-Pruner requires more TFLOPs than Depth Pruning and SliceGPT; however, as shown in Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix D Failure Type Analysis ‣ Persona-Pruner: Sculpting Lightweight Models for Role-Playing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, these baselines frequently produce refusals or incomplete outputs, which can shorten decoding and reduce measured FLOPs.
Compared with the stronger role-playing baselines, LLM-Pruner and Adapt-Pruner, Persona-Pruner achieves higher role-playing performance with lower prefill-plus-decode TFLOPs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A5.T11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 11</span>: </span><span id="A5.T11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Inference efficiency comparison on Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct at 25% sparsity.
We report parameter counts, prefill-only FLOPs, and prefill-plus-decode FLOPs.
Lower is better.
</span></figcaption>
